{"step_index":346,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-24T09:41:34Z","model":"gemini-3.8-flash-high","input_tokens":3636,"cache_read_tokens":177969,"output_tokens":2935,"thinking":"When continuous position analysis is active, the agent must determine if it is the user's turn or if analysis mode is explicitly enabled. If either condition is true, it needs to send the current FEN to Stockfish. The agent needs to choose between a depth-limited search or an infinite search to provide live evaluations. Stockfish will then emit lines containing evaluation information during its calculations.\n\n\nInitial analysis shows Stockfish emits real-time evaluation updates, including depth, score in centipawns or mate score, principal variation, nodes processed, nodes per second, and time elapsed. These updates are provided during both human and AI turns, allowing for continuous evaluation visualization. Correctness requires implementing a turn-aware calculation and display of the evaluation score, specifically converting centipawn scores according to the player's perspective to ensure the score is always from the side-to-move's perspective.\n\n\nDetermining the score from the player's perspective involves inverting the centipawn score if the player is Black, ensuring the evaluation reflects the player's side. The evaluation bar visualization follows standard chess conventions, where the White portion expands with a positive White score and the Black portion with a negative one. Conversion from centipawn scores to a winning percentage for the White player utilizes a sigmoid function.\n\n\nThe sigmoid function translates centipawn scores into a winning percentage, with examples provided for various centipawn values. Mate scores are handled directly, setting the winning percentage to 0% or 100% depending on who has mate. The display labels the bar with mate values like \"M3\" or centipawn values like \"+1.8\".\n\nThe \"Best Move Analysis\" 